WEBVTT

NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:03.660 --> 00:00:06.980
So far, we've just been fitting straight lines to our data.

00:00:06.980 --> 00:00:12.020
Let's take the ideas of multiple linear regression and feature engineering to come

00:00:12.020 --> 00:00:16.660
up with a new algorithm called polynomial regression which will let you fit curves,

00:00:16.660 --> 00:00:18.780
nonlinear functions to your data.

00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:24.060
Let's say you have a housing data set that looks like this, where feature x is the

00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:26.300
size in square feet.

00:00:26.300 --> 00:00:30.220
It doesn't look like a straight line fits this data set very well.

00:00:30.220 --> 00:00:36.860
So maybe you want to fit a curve, maybe a quadratic function to the data like this,

00:00:36.860 --> 00:00:44.500
which includes a size x and also x squared, which is the size raised to the power of 2.

00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:48.180
And maybe that will give you a better fit to the data.

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:51.700
But then you may decide that your quadratic model doesn't really make sense because

00:00:51.700 --> 00:00:55.300
the quadratic function eventually comes back down.

00:00:55.300 --> 00:01:00.180
And well, we wouldn't really expect housing prices to go down when the size increases,

00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:01.180
right?

00:01:01.180 --> 00:01:04.820
Big houses seem like they should usually cost more.

00:01:04.820 --> 00:01:12.580
So then you may choose a cubic function where we now have not only x squared, but x cubed.

00:01:12.580 --> 00:01:18.300
So maybe this model produces this curve here, which is a somewhat better fit to the data

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:23.740
because the size does eventually come back up as the size increases.

00:01:23.740 --> 00:01:29.700
These are both examples of polynomial regression, because you took your optional feature x and

00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:34.740
raised it to the power of 2 or 3 or any other power.

00:01:34.740 --> 00:01:39.420
And in the case of the cubic function, the first feature is the size, the second feature

00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:44.380
is the size squared, and the third feature is the size cubed.

00:01:44.380 --> 00:01:49.140
I just want to point out one more thing, which is that if you create features that

00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:55.300
are these powers, like the square of the original features like this, then feature scaling becomes

00:01:55.300 --> 00:01:57.580
increasingly important.

00:01:57.580 --> 00:02:04.620
So if the size of the house ranges from say 1 to 1000 square feet, then the second feature,

00:02:04.620 --> 00:02:10.620
which is the size squared, would range from 1 to a million, and the third feature, which

00:02:10.620 --> 00:02:15.140
is size cubed, ranges from 1 to a million.

00:02:15.140 --> 00:02:21.340
So these two features, x squared and x cubed, take on very different ranges of values compared

00:02:21.340 --> 00:02:23.180
to the original feature x.

00:02:23.180 --> 00:02:27.980
And if you're using gradient descent, it's important to apply feature scaling to get

00:02:27.980 --> 00:02:31.900
your features into comparable ranges of values.

00:02:31.900 --> 00:02:37.500
Finally, here's one last example of how you really have a wide range of choices of

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:39.540
features to use.

00:02:39.540 --> 00:02:44.300
Another reasonable alternative to taking the size squared and size cubed is to say

00:02:44.300 --> 00:02:46.700
use the square root of x.

00:02:46.700 --> 00:02:55.780
So your model may look like w1 times x plus w2 times the square root of x plus b.

00:02:55.780 --> 00:03:01.900
The square root function looks like this, and it becomes a bit less steep as x increases,

00:03:01.900 --> 00:03:07.740
but it doesn't ever completely flatten out, and it certainly never ever comes back down.

00:03:07.740 --> 00:03:11.580
So this would be another choice of features that might work well for this data set

00:03:11.580 --> 00:03:13.100
as well.

00:03:13.100 --> 00:03:17.740
So you may ask yourself, how do I decide what features to use?

00:03:17.740 --> 00:03:23.380
Later, in the second course in the specialization, you see how you can choose different features

00:03:23.380 --> 00:03:27.260
and different models that include or don't include these features.

00:03:27.260 --> 00:03:32.180
And you have a process for measuring how well these different models perform to help

00:03:32.180 --> 00:03:35.260
you decide which features to include or not include.

00:03:35.260 --> 00:03:40.940
For now, I just want you to be aware that you have a choice in what features you use,

00:03:40.940 --> 00:03:46.100
and by using feature engineering and polynomial functions, you can potentially get a much better

00:03:46.100 --> 00:03:48.900
model for your data.

00:03:48.900 --> 00:03:54.380
In the optional lab that follows this video, you will see some code that implements polynomial

00:03:54.380 --> 00:03:58.620
regression using features like x, x squared, and x cubed.

00:03:58.620 --> 00:04:03.700
So please take a look and run the code and see how it works.

00:04:03.700 --> 00:04:08.380
There's also another optional lab after that one that shows how to use a popular open

00:04:08.380 --> 00:04:13.920
source toolkit that implements linear regression.

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:19.200
Scikit-learn is a very widely used open source machine learning library that is used by many

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:26.830
practitioners in many of the top AI, internet, machine learning companies in the world.

00:04:26.830 --> 00:04:31.190
So if either now or in the future, you're using machine learning in your job, there's

00:04:31.190 --> 00:04:37.670
a very good chance you'll be using tools like Scikit-learn to train your models.

00:04:37.670 --> 00:04:42.150
And so working through that optional lab will give you a chance to not only better understand

00:04:42.510 --> 00:04:47.950
linear regression, but also see how this can be done in just a few lines of code using a

00:04:47.950 --> 00:04:51.230
library like Scikit-learn.

00:04:51.230 --> 00:04:56.710
For you to have a solid understanding of these algorithms and be able to apply them, I do think

00:04:56.710 --> 00:05:01.710
it's important that you know how to implement linear regression yourself and not just call

00:05:01.710 --> 00:05:05.030
some Scikit-learn function that is a black box.

00:05:05.030 --> 00:05:09.790
But Scikit-learn also has an important role in the way machine learning is done in practice

00:05:09.790 --> 00:05:11.910
today.

